  <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
  <h1>The web just started <em>charging</em> AI to read it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">For thirty years the web was free to crawl. That is ending — not gradually, but on a date. The prices and refusals are set machine-to-machine, in headers no one reads. This is the record of them.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="wrap"><div class="panels">

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="ix"><span class="lead-in">The shift</span></div>
    <h2>Reading the web is becoming a transaction, not a right.</h2>
    <p>From 15 September 2026, Cloudflare blocks AI crawlers by default across much of the web and offers publishers a pay-per-crawl toll in the same dashboard. TollBit already runs a bot paywall on thousands of publisher sites. Major outlets have signed private licensing deals with AI companies; others return a payment demand to any crawler that knocks.</p>
    <p>None of this is speculative, and none of it is visible the way markets normally are. There is no ticker. A price quoted to an AI crawler appears for an instant in an HTTP response and is gone. A publisher's policy can change overnight and nobody outside that publisher would know.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="ix"><span class="lead-in">The gap</span></div>
    <p class="pull">Every new market needs a neutral price of record. This one <em>doesn't have one yet.</em></p>
    <p>Oil had no reference price until Platts began publishing assessments; fertiliser had none until Argus did. The reference publisher takes money from no participant — which is exactly what lets everyone cite it. The crawl economy is at that pre-reference moment now: real transactions, no independent record.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="ix"><span class="lead-in">What becomes answerable</span></div>
    <h2>Questions that had no data behind them, now do.</h2>
    <ul class="q-list">
      <li><span class="q">Is my sector walling up faster than the rest of the web?</span><span class="a">Block rates by crawler and by country, re-read weekly across the top 50,000 domains.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">Which publishers have actually started charging — and how much?</span><span class="a">Every observed machine-readable price, and every payment wall that quotes no price, recorded with the date it appeared.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">Did this domain change its AI policy the week that deal was announced?</span><span class="a">A dated history that cannot be back-filled — the one thing a competitor who starts later can never reconstruct.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">Is a licensing deal visible on the wire, or only in the press release?</span><span class="a">Identity-conditional access — sites that serve one AI crawler and refuse another — measured directly.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">How much of the web is one toggle away from charging?</span><span class="a">The share of domains already behind a CDN that can switch on pay-per-crawl in a single setting.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="source">A reporter finds the story; an analyst finds the trend line; a publisher finds out where they stand; an AI team finds out what access will cost. The data is the same — the questions are yours.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="ix"><span class="lead-in">The legal backdrop</span></div>
    <h2>The signal we measure is now the one the law looks at.</h2>
    <p>Whether a site lets AI read it has moved from a technical courtesy to a compliance question, and recent rulings made the machine-readable signal — the thing this index records — the legally operative one.</p>
    <ul class="q-list">
      <li><span class="q">EU: a machine-readable opt-out is the one that counts.</span><span class="a">Under Article 4 of the <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj" rel="noopener">EU Copyright Directive (2019/790)</a>, commercial text-and-data mining is permitted unless rights are reserved by machine-readable means. In <em>Kneschke v. LAION</em> (10 Dec 2025) the Hamburg Higher Regional Court held a natural-language reservation insufficient: the opt-out must be machine-readable — robots.txt, X-Robots-Tag, or the TDM Reservation Protocol. Precisely what this index reads each week.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">EU AI Act: training-data transparency is in force.</span><span class="a">Since 2 August 2025, providers of general-purpose AI models must document training data and comply with EU copyright law, including honouring these reservations. The 2026 Digital Omnibus adjusted some high-risk deadlines; the transparency obligations were not among them.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">A pending EU referral may set the standard web-wide.</span><span class="a">The Court of Justice of the EU is considering case C-250/25 (<em>Like Company v. Google</em>), which may clarify what counts as a valid machine-readable reservation for AI training.</span></li>
      <li><span class="q">US: the market-defining case is still open.</span><span class="a"><em>The New York Times v. OpenAI &amp; Microsoft</em> (S.D.N.Y., 1:23-cv-11195) — core copyright claims survived dismissal; the most-watched test of whether training on copyrighted text is fair use.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="source">We state these as dated facts and link the primary sources. We do not predict how any case or rule will resolve — an observatory records the signal; it does not forecast the verdict.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel wide">
    <div class="ix"><span class="lead-in">What this is</span></div>
    <h2>An independent observatory, not a participant.</h2>
    <p>The Crawl Price Index reads the machine-readable policy of the top 50,000 domains every week and records what it finds: who blocks which AI crawlers, who charges, what the price is, and what changed since last week. It takes no money from anyone it measures and brokers no deals — the discipline that lets a price index be cited by the market it observes.</p>
    <p>Headline figures are free to cite with attribution. The full per-domain record, the country editions, and the accumulating history are the subscription. The method is <a href="/methodology">documented in full</a>, the crawler is cryptographically identifiable, and every edition is dated and reproducible.</p>
  </section>

